Take the number of days in a normal year (365), divide by 7 keeping the
remainder: 1. Now in that span there was 3 leap years giving 3 extra days
(one was actually a leap century which only happens every 400 years). So
you have a total offset of 1x11+3=14 or two weeks. Hence no offset for the
days of the week versus the day number - the years dates fall on the same
weekdays. The moon cycle is 27.32 days. So after 11 years there will be
(11x365.25)/27.32 or 147 lunar cycles and about 2 days extra. So
*technically* it's not a full moon on both days, but close enough. 

